<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">J. M. W. Turner</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1775">1775</a>–<a href="/years/1851">1851</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Joseph Mallord William Turner, William Turner</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joseph Mallord William Turner was an English Romantic painter whose renditions of light, atmosphere and colour in landscape inspired the early impressionists <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-monet">Claude Monet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camille-pissarro">Camille Pissarro</a>. Born in Covent Garden, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, the son of a barber, he was accepted at the Royal Academy of Arts at 14 and became a Royal Academician at 27. He produced about 550 oil paintings and over 2,000 watercolours, and his later, more abstract landscapes, such as Rain, Steam and Speed of 1844, were not well received at the time. He died a rich man in 1851 and bequeathed around 100 finished and 200 unfinished oil paintings to the nation, now shown at the National Gallery and Tate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/j-m-w-turner.jpg" alt="J. M. W. Turner" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: J. M. W. Turner · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turner_selfportrait_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Turner was born on 23 April 1775 on Maiden Lane in Covent Garden, then a working-class district of markets, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a> and taverns. He attended elementary schools in several towns, among them Brentford, Margate and Sunningwell. As a young man he earned a reliable income from topographical and architectural watercolours, worked for architects such as Thomas Hardwick and James Wyatt, studied under the draughtsman Thomas Malton and painted scenery for theatres.</p><p>He entered the Royal Academy Schools in 1789, aged 14, and a watercolour of the Archbishop's Palace at Lambeth was accepted for the Academy's exhibition a year later. By October 1798 he told his friend Joseph Farington that he had 60 watercolour commissions and was earning more than he could spend. Patrons such as Thomas Monro and Walter Fawkes of Farnley Hall supported him, although a few influential critics scorned his working-class origins.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Each year Turner stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> for the Academy's summer exhibition and travelled afterwards, filling nearly 300 sketchbooks that served as sources for later paintings. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-amiens">Peace of Amiens</a> allowed him to see the Old Masters in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> in 1802 and to tour the Swiss Alps. Following Sir Joshua Reynolds, he emulated the Old Masters but made each style his own, and his classical subjects such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dido">Dido</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneas">Aeneas</a> (1814) won praise that set him above them. Several were conceived as warnings against imperial ambition and commercial greed.</p><p>Turner worked to raise landscape in the hierarchy of genres, devoting twelve years to the print series Liber Studiorum, and as Professor of Perspective at the Academy he lectured on landscape technique and the role of poetry in painting. He painted the sea throughout his career and the iron, fire and smoke of the industrial age.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Turner died on 19 December 1851 at the age of 76. His investments and properties were valued at £140,000. He bequeathed to the nation about 100 finished oil paintings, 200 unfinished canvases, over 19,000 sketches, drawings and watercolours and some 200 sketchbooks. His cousins started legal disputes that lasted five years, and in 1856 the Royal Academy received £20,000.</p><p>Many of his late works were once taken for early abstract art, but scholars now largely agree that many were preparatory canvases or studies, although he did simplify shapes and give more weight to light and colour. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-monet">Claude Monet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camille-pissarro">Camille Pissarro</a> saw his paintings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1870 and drew on his use of colour and light. The Turner Prize, created in 1984, carries his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “J. M. W. Turner” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Others prepared the way: the Romantic colourist Eugène Delacroix, <b>J. M. W. Turner</b>, the painters of the Barbizon school such as Théodore Rousseau, and Johan Barthold Jongkind, Jean-Baptiste-Camille Corot and Eugène Boudin, who painted from nature in a direct, spontaneous style and befriended the younger artists.</blockquote><a href="/movement/impressionism">Impressionism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Painters turned to drama and landscape: Friedrich’s Wanderer above the Sea of Fog of about 1818, Théodore Géricault’s The Raft of the Medusa of 1819, the skies of <b>J. M. W. Turner</b> and John Constable.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
